removed theroad subsequent to the City's operations representatives communicating to theHJW
operational team that the City would construct a replacement accessroad subsequent to the
completion of the City's landfill project; however, when the time came, the City renege onits
previous agreement to construt a new access road.
HJWpaid to construct aroad thathadnot previously existed, ie., the roadwasnota pre-existing
City road for which the City paid for and consented forHIW to use.Thus, it isHJW's position
similarly situated road in that the removal of the HJ Access Road was inconsistent with HJ W's
use of the its Access Road pursuant to the Easement, i.e., it was completely removed.In
summary, the parties simply disagree on who is responsible for the expense of a replacement
road, not HJw's ability to construct a replacement access road.HJWreserves theright to resolve
thisissue by the appropriate legal means at somefuture point.
Notwithstanding the above, HJ would like to proceed expeditiously with the construction of a
replacement accessroad.As such, we have requested estimates onthe cost, looking at permitting
and engineering, and the other requisite requirements and approvals for HJW to construct the
